# Hourly check of fseventsd's resident size, with a TERM when the daemon is
# both oversized and hurting the machine (local-fseventsd, sd:8579). On
# 2026-11-11 fseventsd held 28.77 GB resident with swap at 19.70 of 20.48 GB
# and memorystatus pressure level 2, against Apple's own footprint budget of
# about 30 MB, and no vendor fix exists (fleet sd:8557). The nightly health
# check runs the same reading through its status sweep; this job only shortens
# the wait, and it is the one that can repair. FSEVENTSD_MIN_HOURS keeps it to
# one TERM every six hours, so the hourly slot cannot thrash a daemon that
# grows back fast.
#
# Exit 3, nothing to check (FSEVENTSD_RSS_MB unset, not macOS, or no daemon),
# passes as 0: a machine that has not been set up here must not page every
# hour. A refused TERM, a reading that did not answer, a broken setting and a
# floor the tool cannot read exit 1 and fail the run; a run the rate floor
# held back exits 0.
#
# A failing run opens a Reports row that Today shows, and nothing more. It
# sends no push to the phone: every failing path prints `SD_REPORT_ATTENTION:
# data safe, one step failed: fseventsd <step>`, which is what push_at_risk
# reads before it sends its high-priority "data at risk" push. The grading is
# honest and not a mute: the TERM either went out, which is exit 0, or it did
# not, and no failure here risks data. A change that stops printing that line
# wakes the operator at 3am for a false alarm.
JOB_SCHEDULE="25 * * * *"
# A run reads ps and two sysctls and may wait on sudo's askpass, which the
# script itself bounds at 30 s.
JOB_TIMEOUT="2m"
JOB_COMMAND="sh \"$ROOT/../local-fseventsd/fseventsd.sh\" repair || { rc=\$?; [ \"\$rc\" -eq 3 ] || exit \"\$rc\"; }"
